# Carrier Wound #23: The Recovery Path Shared The Failure Mode It Recovers

Wound #22 (Layers A/B/C + the C.2 budget probe) shipped and deployed. Its whole
promise was: **a planner that dies now says why** — pi self-bounds (124 with its
stderr), or the reaper SIGTERMs and the trap emits the cause. The C.2 probe
raised `WORKFLOW_APP_TIMEOUT_MS` to `900000` to absorb the runtime install tax.

Then a fresh dream re-drive blocked terminal again — and the blocker was, once
more, the blind one:

```
Cloudflare Sandbox lane did not emit a result marker (exit 1).
```

`nodes=0`, no pi stderr, no failing step. Wound #22 was supposed to make this
sentence unreachable. It wasn't — because #22 made the *failure trap fire*; it
never asked whether the **trap's own machinery** could survive the conditions
that tripped it. This is the recovery-class wound in its purest form, and the
sixth-plus that the live-observe mandate caught from the outside-invisible /
node-by-node-legible gap.

## Mechanism — two coupled halves, one shared root: node dies with the lane

### Wound #23-A — `emit_failure_marker` builds its marker with `node`

The EXIT/SIGNAL trap (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`) captures pi's
stderr tail + git log tail and hands them to a `node` heredoc that JSON-escapes
the arbitrary text and base64s the marker:

```sh
PIWF_FAIL_STDERR_TAIL="$stderr_tail" ... node <<'NODE'
const payload = { status: "error", exitCode: ..., stderrTail: ... };
process.stdout.write("\n__PIWF_AGENT_LANE_RESULT__:" + Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(payload), "utf8").toString("base64") + "\n");
NODE
```

node is correct for *escaping* — but node is **also the single most
memory-hungry actor on a `lite` sandbox**, and the lane's dominant failure mode
is resource exhaustion (see #23-B). When the lane OOMs, the recovery that is
supposed to *report* the OOM is `node` — which **cannot fork / cannot allocate /
gets OOM-killed mid-write**. The trap fires, runs, and emits **nothing**. The
parser sees no marker and throws the blind sentence. The application that was
taught to announce its failure lost its voice at exactly the moment it had
something to announce.

> A Philosophy of Software Design (John Ousterhout), Ch. on exception handling:
>
> > Secondary exceptions occurring during recovery are often more subtle and
> > complex than the primary exceptions.

Ousterhout cites Yuan et al. (OSDI '14): *the majority of catastrophic failures
in distributed, data-intensive systems were caused by incorrect handling of
non-fatal errors* — the recovery code, not the original fault, is what takes the
system down. That is this wound exactly: the primary exception (the OOM grind)
was survivable as a blocker; the **secondary** exception (node can't emit under
that same pressure) is what produced the catastrophic *causeless* block.

This is the [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]] antidote stated structurally:
**recovery must not share the failure mode of the failure it recovers.** A node
that needs a V8 heap to report "we ran out of heap" is a polite recovery; the
hostile-real recovery uses only what survives the fire.

### Wound #23-B — the normalizer is O(n²) and OOMs the lite instance

`extractFirstJsonValueText` (and its embedded node-script twin in
`agent-lane-json-output.ts`) outer-loops over **every** character; at each `{` or
`[` it calls `findJsonEnd` (a full forward scan) + `JSON.parse`. A real verdict
parses on the first opener and stops. But a **truncated or prose-heavy** pi blob
full of unbalanced openers re-scans forward from every one of them — O(n²).
Measured on the actual extractor:

| input size | time |
|-----------:|-----:|
| 116 KB | 451 ms |
| 477 KB | 9 003 ms |
| 1.2 MB | 59 105 ms |

A ~1 MB pathological output grinds for a full minute on the `lite` sandbox,
burns the lane budget, **and the grind's allocation pressure is what OOMs the
instance** — which is what then kills the #23-A node recovery. #23-B is the
*primary* exception; #23-A is why the primary one became invisible. They had to
be fixed together.

## Fix — recover with what survives the fire; bound the grind to O(n)

### Fix A — shell-only fallback marker + parser that steps past corpses

**(1) node touches a sentinel as its LAST act; absent sentinel ⇒ shell-only
fallback** (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`):

```sh
marker_ok_path="/workspace/.piwf-marker-ok"
rm -f "$marker_ok_path" 2>/dev/null || true
PIWF_MARKER_OK_PATH="$marker_ok_path" ... node <<'NODE'
...process.stdout.write("\n__PIWF_AGENT_LANE_RESULT__:" + ...);
try { require("fs").writeFileSync(process.env.PIWF_MARKER_OK_PATH, "1"); } catch (error) {}
NODE
  if [ ! -f "$marker_ok_path" ]; then
    fallback_pi_status="$pi_status"; [ -z "$fallback_pi_status" ] && fallback_pi_status="null"
    fallback_json="$(printf '{"status":"error","exitCode":%s,"failingStep":"%s","gitLogTail":"","piStatus":%s,"stderrTail":"[degraded recovery: node-based result marker emit failed (likely resource exhaustion); pi and git tails unavailable]"}' "$exit_code" "$current_step" "$fallback_pi_status")"
    fallback_b64="$(printf '%s' "$fallback_json" | base64 2>/dev/null | tr -d '\n')"
    [ -n "$fallback_b64" ] && printf '\n__PIWF_AGENT_LANE_RESULT__:%s\n' "$fallback_b64"
  fi
```

The sentinel write is node's final statement; if the marker reached stdout the
sentinel exists. Absent sentinel = node failed *after or during* the emit, so a
**shell-only** path emits a minimal-but-valid `status:"error"` marker. It uses
only the `printf` builtin and the tiny `base64` coreutil — **no V8 heap**, the
one thing guaranteed available when node is dead. Only known-safe fields are
interpolated (numeric `exit_code`, numeric-or-`null` `pi_status`, the fixed
`[a-z-]` step token `current_step`), so no JSON escaping is needed and the
payload is always valid. The degraded-recovery note rides in `stderrTail` — a
field the marker schema keeps — so `selectLaneAbortDiagnostic` surfaces it as
the blocker's **primary cause** instead of the blind sentence.

**(2) `parseLaneResultMarker` scans ALL markers, returns the first that
validates.** A node OOM-killed mid-`stdout.write` can leave a truncated
`__PIWF_AGENT_LANE_RESULT__:<partial>` line; the shell fallback then appends a
*valid* marker after it. The old first-match `.exec` would lock onto the corpse
and throw, masking the recovered cause:

```ts
const markers = result.stdout.matchAll(/__PIWF_AGENT_LANE_RESULT__:([A-Za-z0-9+/=]+)/gu);
let sawMarker = false;
for (const marker of markers) {
  const [, encoded] = marker;
  if (encoded === undefined) continue;
  sawMarker = true;
  const parsed = SandboxLaneResultMarkerSchema.safeParse(tryDecodeMarkerJson(encoded));
  if (parsed.success) return parsed.data;
}
throw new Error(`Cloudflare Sandbox lane ${sawMarker ? "emitted only unparseable result markers" : "did not emit a result marker"} ...`);
```

`tryDecodeMarkerJson` returns `null` on any malformed base64/JSON so the loop
steps to the next marker instead of throwing on the partial. The error message
now even distinguishes *no marker at all* from *only-corrupt markers* — two
different carrier states that used to read identically.

### Fix B — bound the scan to O(n), fail fast as `oversized_output`

A single scan budget, linear in input, applied in **both** copies of
`extractFirstJsonValueText`:

```js
let scanBudget = raw.length * 4 + 1_000_000;
// inside findJsonEnd's inner loop:
scanBudget -= 1;
if (scanBudget <= 0) {
  const oversized = new RangeError("Agent lane output exceeded the JSON scan budget; ...");
  oversized.code = "OVERSIZED_OUTPUT";
  throw oversized;
}
```

`4n + 1M` is generous enough that **any single valid value — however large —
passes in one forward scan**; only the quadratic re-scan from many unbalanced
openers trips it, and it trips almost immediately. `tryExtract` re-throws
`OVERSIZED_OUTPUT` (it is NOT a "no JSON here ⇒ null" fall-through — it means
*this* text is pathological, so abort the whole normalize rather than silently
re-grind the budget on the next event). The outcome mapping adds a distinct
`reason: "oversized_output"`. A multi-minute OOM grind becomes a sub-second,
honest failure the marker can actually carry — which is also what keeps node
*alive* to carry it.

## Chaos Receipts (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — **30 passing**:

- **#23-A hostile double** (`describe(buildPiAgentLaneCommand)`): slices the real
  `emit_failure_marker` block out of `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()`, **shims `node`
  to `exit 137`** (the OOM-killer's signal), sets `current_step=normalize-output`,
  `pi_status=0`, `exit 137`, runs it under `bash`. Asserts the SIGKILL'd node
  emitted **no** rich marker (`nodeNeverWroteRichMarker`) yet the lane still
  produced **exactly one** valid `status:"error"` marker (`exactlyOneMarker`)
  carrying the degraded-recovery note (`carriesDegradedRecoveryNote`), the right
  failing step (`namesFailingStep:"normalize-output"`), and the preserved exit
  (137) + pi status (0). It reproduces prod transport — real trap, real shell
  fallback, real base64 — and fakes only node's death. Before Fix A this emitted
  zero markers: the exact blind production blocker.
- **#23-B hostile double** (`describe(normalizeAgentLaneJsonOutput)`): feeds
  `"{".repeat(600_000)` (the truncated-opener pathology) through the real
  embedded node-script normalizer. Asserts it `completedWellUnderHang`
  (`elapsedMs < 5000` — vs the ~59 s the unbounded version would grind),
  `failedExit:true`, `laneOutput:null`, `normalized:false`,
  `reason:"oversized_output"`. Before Fix B this ran for ~minutes and would have
  OOM'd a real lite instance.

The doubles are hostile by construction (the [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]
discipline): a polite mock of node would have *returned a marker* and masked
#23-A entirely; a polite normalizer test would have used a *valid* small blob and
never tripped the quadratic. Both deliberately reproduce the production failure
mode.

## Verification

```bash
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts  # 30 pass (2 new)
pnpm typecheck   # clean (tsgo)
pnpm check       # oxfmt + oxlint + type-aware, clean
```

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. Fix A+B
deploy, then ONE dream-shape run is watched node-by-node. The wound class is now
closed from both ends: the normalizer can no longer OOM the instance on a
pathological blob (B), and even if *something else* exhausts memory, the failure
marker no longer needs a V8 heap to report it (A) — and a truncated partial
marker can no longer mask the good one (A.2). There is no remaining path by which
the planner blocks with `did not emit a result marker` and no cause: either the
marker is valid (rich, from node) or valid (minimal, from the shell), and the
parser walks past any corpse to find it.

This is the recovery-machinery sibling of [[carrier-wound-lane-self-collision-2026-06-14]]:
#22 taught the lane to *announce* on the way down; #23 made sure the announcer
itself survives the fire. Following [[carrier-wound-btoa-latin1-ceiling-2026-06-14]],
all of them surfaced only because the run was watched node-by-node, not stared at.
Captured on deployed code → fire the operator Discord ping with the wzrrd HITL
URL, reset D3, start the 10-run burn-in.
